   147	### 4.2 ping
   148	
   149	Application-level heartbeat. The server MUST respond with a `pong` message echoing the timestamp.
   150	
   151	```json
   152	{
   153	  "type": "ping",
   154	  "timestamp": 1705312200000
   155	}
   156	```
   157	
   158	| Field       | Type   | Required | Description                      |
   159	| ----------- | ------ | -------- | -------------------------------- |
   160	| `type`      | string | Yes      | `"ping"`                         |
   161	| `timestamp` | number | Yes      | Client timestamp in milliseconds |
   162	
   163	### 4.3 list_sessions
   164	
   165	Asks for the connecting user's own conversation summaries. The frame never carries a user id: the server derives it from the authenticated connection, ignores unknown fields, and forwards only the optional `limit` and `before` cursor to the store. It accepts a non-negative integer `limit` and a `before` object whose `sessionId` is 1–128 characters and whose `updatedAt` is an ISO timestamp or epoch number; an invalid field receives `INVALID_MESSAGE`.
   166	
   167	```json
   168	{
   169	  "type": "list_sessions",
   170	  "limit": 50,
   171	  "before": {"updatedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "sessionId": "s1"}
   172	}
   173	```
   174	
   175	| Field    | Type                     | Required | Description                                     |
   176	| -------- | ------------------------ | -------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
   177	| `type`   | string                   | Yes      | `"list_sessions"`                               |
   178	| `limit`  | number                   | No       | Maximum number of summaries to return           |
   179	| `before` | `{updatedAt, sessionId}` | No       | Cursor from the final row of the preceding page |
   180	
   181	The server answers this frame off the per-session turn lock, so it can return while a user message is streaming. Listing is available when a store supplies `listFor`; a store without it answers an empty `session_list`, while a rejected list answers `SERVER_ERROR` and leaves the socket open.
   182	
   183	## 5. Server-to-Client Messages
   184	
   185	### 5.1 connected
   186	
   187	Sent exactly once after a successful handshake. See Section 2.3.
   188	
   189	| Field            | Type     | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
   190	| ---------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   191	| `type`           | string   | `"connected"`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
   192	| `sessionId`      | string   | UUID v4 identifying the session                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
   193	| `message`        | string   | Human-readable greeting                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   194	| `examplePrompts` | string[] | Example queries derived from registered tool definitions                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
   195	| `resumed`        | boolean  | Present only with resume enabled: whether the requested conversation was rejoined                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
   196	| `entryCount`     | number   | Present only when `resumed` is `true`: how many stored entries the replay covers, skipped ones included                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   197	| `resumeFailure`  | string   | Present only when the connection named an id and `resumed` is `false`: `expired` when the caller's own session aged out, `unknown` otherwise. `unknown` deliberately covers both an id the server never issued and one that is not the caller's, so the answer reveals nothing about ids that exist |
   198	
   199	### 5.2 entry_upsert
   200	
   201	Creates a new entry or replaces an existing entry at the given index. This is the primary mechanism for adding content to the conversation.
   202	
   203	```json
   204	{
   205	  "type": "entry_upsert",
   206	  "index": 0,
   207	  "entry": {
   208	    "role": "user",
   209	    "content": "What is the weather in Berlin?",
   210	    "timestamp": "2026-01-15T14:30:00Z"
   211	  }
   212	}
   213	```
   214	
   215	| Field   | Type         | Description                            |
   216	| ------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------- |
   217	| `type`  | string       | `"entry_upsert"`                       |
   218	| `index` | number       | Zero-based position in the entry array |
   219	| `entry` | SessionEntry | The entry object (see Section 6)       |
   220	
   340	- Any other failure raised while processing a message is reported as `SERVER_ERROR` ([validated by: reports any other failure as a server error](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L356)).
   341	
   342	### 5.7 pong
   343	
   344	Response to a client `ping`. The server MUST echo the client's timestamp without modification ([validated by: answers a ping with the timestamp it was given](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L82)).
   345	
   346	```json
   347	{
   348	  "type": "pong",
   349	  "timestamp": 1705312200000
   350	}
   351	```
   352	
   353	| Field       | Type   | Description                             |
   354	| ----------- | ------ | --------------------------------------- |
   355	| `type`      | string | `"pong"`                                |
   356	| `timestamp` | number | Echoed from the client's `ping` message |
   357	
   358	### 5.8 session_list
   359	
   360	Answers `list_sessions` with the authenticated user's conversation metadata, in the store's newest-activity-first order. It is sent only in response to a request, never pushed. Every row contains metadata only — no entry content or credentials — and dates are ISO-8601 strings so they survive JSON serialization.
   361	
   362	```json
   363	{
   364	  "type": "session_list",
   365	  "sessions": [
   366	    {
   367	      "sessionId": "s1",
   368	      "createdAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
   369	      "updatedAt": "2026-01-01T00:02:00.000Z",
   370	      "entryCount": 4
   371	    }
   372	  ]
   373	}
   374	```
   375	
   376	| Field      | Type               | Description                                              |
   377	| ---------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |
   378	| `type`     | string             | `"session_list"`                                         |
   379	| `sessions` | `SessionSummary[]` | One authenticated user's metadata-only conversation rows |
   380	
   381	A client reopens a selected row by reconnecting with `?sessionId=<sessionId>` when resume is enabled; the existing resume ownership check remains the only path that authorises that replay.
   382	
   383	### 5.9 stream_end
   384	
   385	Signals that the server has finished processing a user message. Sent exactly once for every `user_message` the server receives, after all `entry_commit` messages have been sent, whether the run succeeded or failed.
   386	
   387	```json
   388	{
   389	  "type": "stream_end"
   390	}
   391	```
   392	
   393	| Field  | Type   | Description    |
   394	| ------ | ------ | -------------- |
   395	| `type` | string | `"stream_end"` |
   396	
   397	Semantics:
   398	
   399	- The server MUST send `stream_end` after every `user_message` it receives, including streams where output was suppressed (see [tool-responses.md](../hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md)), streams that failed after an `error` frame, and a `user_message` that failed validation ([single-terminal-frame](../hal-engine-single-terminal-frame/spec.md)). The one exception is a turn whose socket closes first: it is abandoned, and nothing more is sent ([abandon-on-close](../hal-engine-abandon-on-close/spec.md)).
   400	- `stream_end` follows every stream even after an error: a failed run sends its `error` frame first and `stream_end` last.
   401	- An `error` frame does not by itself end a stream. It is advisory: it may concern the run in flight or an unrelated frame, such as a malformed `ping`.
   402	- The client SHOULD use this message to clear any "processing" or "loading" indicators.
   403	- The client MUST treat a run as complete only at `stream_end` or when the socket closes. Unparseable JSON is the one frame answered with `INVALID_FORMAT` and no `stream_end`, because the server cannot tell what it was meant to be; for it the client falls back on the socket closing.
   404	
   405	## 6. SessionEntry Objects
   406	
   407	A SessionEntry represents a single piece of content in the conversation. Every entry has a `role` field that determines its shape. The `role` acts as a discriminant for the union type.
   408	
   409	### 6.1 UserEntry
   410	
   411	A message authored by the user.
   412	
   413	```json
   414	{
   415	  "role": "user",
   416	  "content": "What is the weather in Berlin?",
   417	  "timestamp": "2026-01-15T14:30:00Z"
   418	}
   419	```
   420	
   421	| Field       | Type   | Description       |
   422	| ----------- | ------ | ----------------- |
   423	| `role`      | string | `"user"`          |
   424	| `content`   | string | The message text  |
   425	| `timestamp` | string | ISO 8601 datetime |
   426	
   427	### 6.2 AssistantEntry
   428	
   429	A response generated by the AI model. Content is delivered incrementally via `entry_delta` messages while `isStreaming` is `true`.
   430	
